    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The most extensive of the first urban civilisations, it covered more ground than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> together, with over a thousand known sites and great cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a>.</li><li>Its builders laid out streets on a grid, used bricks of standard proportions and drained whole neighbourhoods with covered sewers, yet left no palaces, royal tombs or images of war.</li><li>Its short inscriptions remain undeciphered, so its language, rulers and beliefs are known only through archaeology and through Mesopotamian texts that call it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meluhha">Meluhha</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A cylinder seal of the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, belonged to a man named Shu-ilishu, who called himself “interpreter of the Meluhhan language”: Mesopotamians needed translators to deal with traders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

